1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,780
Imagine you run one of the biggest
technology companies on Earth.

2
00:00:04,448 --> 00:00:05,428
Thousands of engineers.

3
00:00:05,658 --> 00:00:08,068
Billions of dollars.

4
00:00:08,613 --> 00:00:11,133
And one of the most important pieces
of your infrastructure

5
00:00:11,363 --> 00:00:14,613
is controlled by a man
who doesn't work for you.

6
00:00:15,087 --> 00:00:17,887
You can't fire him.
You can't overrule him.

7
00:00:18,469 --> 00:00:21,249
When your engineers want to change
that software,

8
00:00:21,329 --> 00:00:24,209
they send him their work,
and wait for his answer.

9
00:00:24,773 --> 00:00:26,413
That man is Linus Torvalds.

10
00:00:26,643 --> 00:00:28,773
And the software is Linux.

11
00:00:29,585 --> 00:00:34,205
Google does this.
So do Microsoft, Intel, Amazon and Meta.

12
00:00:34,953 --> 00:00:37,313
So why hasn't Big Tech replaced him?

13
00:00:39,713 --> 00:00:43,413
Linux began as a student's hobby
in Helsinki in 1991.

14
00:00:44,313 --> 00:00:48,833
Today, it runs Android phones,
cloud servers, and all five hundred

15
00:00:48,936 --> 00:00:51,453
of the world's fastest supercomputers.

16
00:00:52,184 --> 00:00:55,704
The latest release brought
almost nineteen thousand changes,

17
00:00:55,924 --> 00:00:59,644
from about three thousand developers
and more than two hundred companies.

18
00:01:00,251 --> 00:01:03,491
So how many of those changes
did Linus write himself?

19
00:01:04,421 --> 00:01:05,621
Nine.

20
00:01:06,287 --> 00:01:09,167
And eight of them
just changed the version number.

21
00:01:09,796 --> 00:01:10,836
In two weeks, he merged

22
00:01:11,066 --> 00:01:13,676
around two hundred and forty batches
of changes,

23
00:01:13,996 --> 00:01:16,536
each one sent to him by someone he trusts.

24
00:01:17,316 --> 00:01:20,396
He's in charge
because the code ends up with him.

25
00:01:20,632 --> 00:01:24,892
And that's exactly the part a company
like Google could simply walk away from.

26
00:01:27,272 --> 00:01:29,112
Linux is open source.

27
00:01:29,832 --> 00:01:32,692
Anyone can take the code
and make their own version.

28
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:36,430
It's called a fork,
and you don't need Linus's permission.

29
00:01:37,322 --> 00:01:39,242
Google did exactly that.

30
00:01:39,635 --> 00:01:41,195
Not a whole new Linux.

31
00:01:42,015 --> 00:01:44,215
It took the kernel,
the core that talks to the hardware,

32
00:01:44,445 --> 00:01:46,855
and kept its own branch of it for Android.

33
00:01:47,669 --> 00:01:52,729
In December 2009, Android's code
was removed from the main kernel.

34
00:01:53,397 --> 00:01:54,497
Greg Kroah-Hartman,

35
00:01:54,577 --> 00:01:57,277
one of the senior maintainers,
explained why:

36
00:01:57,457 --> 00:02:00,117
no one cared about the code,
so it was removed.

37
00:02:00,617 --> 00:02:02,917
So Android's kernel drifted away.

38
00:02:03,636 --> 00:02:07,596
Chip makers and phone makers
added their own changes on top.

39
00:02:08,191 --> 00:02:10,731
By Google's own account,
up to half the code

40
00:02:10,961 --> 00:02:14,071
in a phone's kernel
could come from outside the main kernel.

41
00:02:14,465 --> 00:02:17,005
And every security fix found in Linux

42
00:02:17,105 --> 00:02:19,845
had to be carried over
into each of those versions.

43
00:02:20,599 --> 00:02:24,079
Google's documentation calls that
"prohibitively expensive".

44
00:02:25,047 --> 00:02:29,007
A new kernel release could take
up to eighteen months to reach a phone.

45
00:02:30,223 --> 00:02:34,043
Two years after deleting
Android's code, Greg put it back.

46
00:02:34,692 --> 00:02:38,492
His note said: "Turns out I was wrong,
we want these in the tree."

47
00:02:38,961 --> 00:02:42,141
And since Android 12,
phones that launch on a newer kernel

48
00:02:42,371 --> 00:02:46,781
must use one common kernel from Google,
the Generic Kernel Image.

49
00:02:47,463 --> 00:02:50,643
It's still Linux
with Android's own changes on top.

50
00:02:51,003 --> 00:02:55,443
But it stays close to the main version,
because that's where the fixes are.

51
00:02:56,009 --> 00:02:58,269
Making your own version is easy.

52
00:02:58,789 --> 00:03:01,409
Keeping it alive is the expensive part.

53
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:05,720
That's why companies send their code
to the main kernel.

54
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:11,040
Not as a favour. Once it's in,
everyone else helps look after it.

55
00:03:13,370 --> 00:03:16,190
But Linus can't read
nineteen thousand changes.

56
00:03:17,090 --> 00:03:18,290
Nobody could.

57
00:03:18,588 --> 00:03:25,108
Linux is split into areas. Networking.
Memory. File systems. Graphics drivers.

58
00:03:25,188 --> 00:03:26,788
Each processor family.

59
00:03:27,371 --> 00:03:29,891
Each area has its own maintainers.

60
00:03:30,451 --> 00:03:33,811
The list of them has around
three and a half thousand entries.

61
00:03:34,509 --> 00:03:37,409
A change goes first
to the right maintainer.

62
00:03:37,890 --> 00:03:42,350
They review it, argue about it,
ask for fixes, and collect what's ready.

63
00:03:42,714 --> 00:03:44,414
Then they send it up.

64
00:03:44,983 --> 00:03:48,483
At the top, more than a hundred of them
send their collections to Linus.

65
00:03:49,431 --> 00:03:51,111
Linux is built everywhere.

66
00:03:51,191 --> 00:03:53,711
But it comes together in one place.

67
00:03:56,061 --> 00:03:59,281
Now, the companies
sending all that code aren't charities.

68
00:04:00,210 --> 00:04:03,170
These companies compete
with each other every day.

69
00:04:03,805 --> 00:04:08,585
Would Google let Microsoft decide what
goes into the software its servers run on?

70
00:04:09,127 --> 00:04:11,347
Linus doesn't work for any of them.

71
00:04:11,675 --> 00:04:13,375
So who does pay him?

72
00:04:14,054 --> 00:04:15,414
The Linux Foundation.

73
00:04:16,518 --> 00:04:20,978
And the Foundation isn't a charity
standing outside the corporate world.

74
00:04:21,563 --> 00:04:23,743
It's an association of companies.

75
00:04:24,582 --> 00:04:27,722
Google, Microsoft, Intel,
Meta, Amazon and others

76
00:04:27,952 --> 00:04:32,162
pay for its top membership,
and each of them gets a seat on its board.

77
00:04:32,806 --> 00:04:36,806
The Foundation says it pays Linus
so that he can stay independent.

78
00:04:37,787 --> 00:04:40,547
That's a stated purpose,
not a published guarantee.

79
00:04:41,489 --> 00:04:44,469
The Foundation hosts
more than a thousand projects.

80
00:04:45,229 --> 00:04:47,929
The kernel gets about three percent
of its budget.

81
00:04:48,369 --> 00:04:51,369
So the companies aren't putting
one of their own at the top.

82
00:04:52,007 --> 00:04:54,627
They're supporting a place
where they can work together,

83
00:04:54,707 --> 00:04:56,647
without any one of them in charge.

84
00:04:59,547 --> 00:05:02,287
But does Linus really have the final word?

85
00:05:02,907 --> 00:05:04,687
Here's what that looks like.

86
00:05:04,993 --> 00:05:09,633
In 2024, Linux got
a new file system called bcachefs,

87
00:05:09,973 --> 00:05:12,353
a new way of storing files on a disk.

88
00:05:13,003 --> 00:05:14,923
Every new version has a rule.

89
00:05:15,423 --> 00:05:18,043
New features come in
during the first two weeks.

90
00:05:18,783 --> 00:05:20,403
After that, only fixes.

91
00:05:21,274 --> 00:05:25,114
Its developer kept sending
large changes after those two weeks.

92
00:05:25,765 --> 00:05:28,465
In August 2024, Linus wrote:

93
00:05:28,810 --> 00:05:31,930
"At some point, 'fix something'
just turns into development.

94
00:05:32,710 --> 00:05:34,150
And this is that point."

95
00:05:35,281 --> 00:05:37,721
The arguments went on for another year.

96
00:05:38,321 --> 00:05:42,701
In 2025, Linus took bcachefs
out of the kernel.

97
00:05:43,367 --> 00:05:45,607
It still exists, as a separate add-on.

98
00:05:45,687 --> 00:05:48,287
Just not in the shared version.

99
00:05:49,105 --> 00:05:53,425
Linus has power because the people
who send him code keep trusting him.

100
00:05:56,165 --> 00:05:58,605
So what happens when Linus is gone?

101
00:05:59,511 --> 00:06:01,171
We already know a little.

102
00:06:01,514 --> 00:06:04,654
In September 2018, Linus stepped away.

103
00:06:05,322 --> 00:06:08,322
He apologised for attacking people
in his emails,

104
00:06:08,542 --> 00:06:11,202
and said he needed time
to change how he behaved.

105
00:06:12,031 --> 00:06:15,771
Greg Kroah-Hartman, the same maintainer
from the Android story,

106
00:06:15,851 --> 00:06:18,071
took over
and finished the next version, 4.19.

107
00:06:19,701 --> 00:06:21,281
Linux kept going.

108
00:06:21,741 --> 00:06:24,041
A few weeks later, Linus came back.

109
00:06:25,327 --> 00:06:28,367
Since 2026, there's even a written plan.

110
00:06:28,892 --> 00:06:32,592
If Linus can't continue,
the senior maintainers start talking

111
00:06:32,822 --> 00:06:35,652
within seventy-two hours
about who takes over.

112
00:06:36,177 --> 00:06:37,877
It doesn't name a successor.

113
00:06:38,497 --> 00:06:40,457
It describes how to find one.

114
00:06:40,833 --> 00:06:42,953
So Linux can survive without Linus.

115
00:06:43,801 --> 00:06:46,361
What it can't easily replace
is the trust that put him

116
00:06:46,591 --> 00:06:48,361
at the centre in the first place.

117
00:06:51,661 --> 00:06:54,861
So why hasn't Big Tech
replaced Linus Torvalds?

118
00:06:55,519 --> 00:06:58,219
Because what they'd be replacing
isn't a programmer.

119
00:06:58,978 --> 00:07:01,198
It's the one place
where all their competing code

120
00:07:01,428 --> 00:07:05,158
comes together, looked after by someone
none of them owns.

121
00:07:05,901 --> 00:07:07,781
Linus doesn't own the kingdom.

122
00:07:08,401 --> 00:07:12,481
Everyone just agrees that, for now,
he's the right person to guard it.

123
00:07:14,611 --> 00:07:16,491
So here's a question for you.

124
00:07:17,151 --> 00:07:19,351
When Linus does step down,
should one person

125
00:07:19,581 --> 00:07:22,311
get the final word again,
or should it be a group?

126
00:07:23,122 --> 00:07:24,552
Let me know in the comments.
